                    {
                        "sense_id": "83458ec0-61fc-3273-abed-0e7e5cf21149",
                        "entry_id": "cbdf43aa-25cd-3d9c-891b-957268b3eae2",
                        "glosses": [
                            "King of Thebes, noted primarily in the stories of Antigone and Oedipus."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "Greek"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "ad07a3d5-4eb8-4717-9224-165d90d11c30",
                                "sense_id": "83458ec0-61fc-3273-abed-0e7e5cf21149",
                                "text": "It has often been observed that Creon imputes corrupt motives (here and to Teiresias), because this was a level of motivation within his comprehension."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "b5f6a18b-2f36-48c1-9709-1ee1e3694601",
                                "sense_id": "83458ec0-61fc-3273-abed-0e7e5cf21149",
                                "text": "Above all -- and this is of central importance in connection with the legal implications -- it is essential to understand that Creon must emerge as an appropriate counterweight to the obsessive energy of Antigone: that \"Antigone's fate provides the foil for Creon and, in turn, Creon's fate becomes evident only against the background of Antigone's desitiny."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "05a7c5eb-0f65-4629-ba3a-777f20065f35",
                                "sense_id": "83458ec0-61fc-3273-abed-0e7e5cf21149",
                                "text": "Perhaps more than any other figure in the Oedipus Trilogy, Creon, Oedipus' brother-in-law, seems to be a very different character in each of the plays."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
